  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 100 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAfternoon Storm Chances Wednesday: The upper level ridge will\nslowly begin to retrograde towards the area which equates to\nrising mid level heights. This is not helpful for storm\ndevelopment. However if that weak boundary/wind shift can make\nit into the area then it may be enough to force some scattered \nshowers and storms in the afternoon. The latest REFS shows a \nconsensus of storms from the Branson area to Springfield then up\ntowards Rolla (roughly where that boundary may end up or wash\nout). Currently thinking only a handful of storms and have kept\npops at or below 20 percent. The lack of lift is the main \nlimiting factor at this time for higher pops. The strongest \nstorms on Wednesday could produce wind gusts up to 50mph given\nincreasing PW values around 1.75in. Many areas will remain dry\nhowever.\n\nHeat Advisory Extended: Ensembles suggest that the upper high\nwill slowly retrograde west mid to late week. This will keep\n850mb temps in the 20-24C range which will continue to keep \nsurface temperatures 15+ degrees above average. Daily highs in \nthe middle to upper 90s are likely with lows in the 70s. Some \nafternoon mixing will occur however daily heat index values \naround 100 will continue. The Heat Risk tool continues to \nsupport the potential for major to extreme heat impacts. This is\nlikely due to the prolonged heat aspect and the climatological \naspect. All this to say that the Heat Advisory has been extended\nthrough 8pm Friday for the entire area. \n\nPattern Change Late Weekend? Ensembles then suggest that\nstronger shortwave energy will push into the Rockies late in the\nweekend. This will allow for decreasing mid level heights and\ntemps along with a frontal passage at some point. The timing of\nthis front remains in question however there is cooler air\nbehind it. Currently precip chances are 30-50% Sunday night into\nMonday with that frontal passage. NBM temp spreads do increase \nduring this timeframe however even the higher percentiles would \nindicate highs in the 80s which would be welcome.",
